# ldap — real-world examples

## Install LDAP client stack (RHEL/CentOS lab example)

```sh
yum install nss-pam-ldapd pam_krb5 autofs nfs-utils openldap-clients
```

## Configure LDAP auth with authconfig (RHEL 6/7 era)

```sh
authconfig --enableldap --enableldapauth --enablemkhomedir --enableldaptls \
  --ldaploadcacert=http://ldap.linuxacademy.com/pub/cert.pem \
  --ldapserver=ldap.linuxacademy.com \
  --ldapbasedn="dc=linuxacademy,dc=com" --update
```

## GUI version of the same

```sh
export XAUTHORITY=/home/cloud_user/.Xauthority
yum install authconfig-gtk
authconfig-gtk
```

## Query a directory with ldapsearch

```sh
# Anonymous search for a user entry
ldapsearch -x -H ldap://ldap.example.com -b "dc=example,dc=com" "(uid=david)"

# Authenticated search, prompt for password
ldapsearch -x -H ldaps://ldap.example.com -D "cn=admin,dc=example,dc=com" -W \
  -b "ou=people,dc=example,dc=com" "(objectClass=posixAccount)" uid
```

## Homelab: OpenSUSE Tumbleweed equivalents

```sh
sudo zypper install openldap2-client sssd
# Modern setups use sssd rather than nss-pam-ldapd; config lives in
# /etc/sssd/sssd.conf, then:
sudo systemctl enable --now sssd
```

## Homelab: test that LDAP accounts resolve

```sh
getent passwd david        # should return the LDAP entry if NSS is wired up
id david
```

| Tool / flag      | What it does                          |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| ldapsearch -x    | simple (non-SASL) bind                |
| -H <uri>         | server URI (ldap:// or ldaps://)      |
| -b <basedn>      | search base                           |
| -D <binddn> -W   | bind DN, prompt for password          |
| getent passwd    | verify NSS integration                |
